in late May three months after the U.S  imposed sanctions on Russian oil  gasoline made from Russian crude was  delivered to this terminal in New Jersey  it was later distributed to gas stations  across the East Coast  it came from this refinery in Italy  owned by Russian oil giant Luke oil  that's because of a U.S sanctions  loophole that allows banned Russian  crude to be refined in a country outside  of Russia and sent to the U.S  we tracked ships pieced together months  of commodity data shipping documents and  satellite imagery to reveal how Russia's  second largest oil and gas company Luke  oil is sending Russian oil refined in  Italy to companies like Exxon in the  United States without violating  sanctions  our investigation shows that Luke oil's  isab refinery in Syracuse Sicily  represents one of the most extreme  examples of this loophole the vast  majority of the crude for this Refinery  comes from Russia it's then refined in  Sicily  after which it's considered Italian  thanks to a long-standing industry  practice that allows the origin of oil  to change based on where it's refined  the U.S launched sanctions against  Russia as punishment for invading  Ukraine in late February Banning Imports  of Russian oil and oil products as well  as blacklisting Russian oligarchs and  companies the U.S says are close to  President Putin  unlike Russia's largest state-owned oil  and gas company rosnaft Luke oil was not  sanctioned  Luke oil still has a presence in the U.S  and distributes oil products to 11  states according to the company's  website  the only reason it can sell Russian oil  into the U.S is because it stops at the  refinery in Sicily on its way here  Luke oil's refinery in Sicily is the  second largest in Italy and the fifth  largest in Europe  before the war it brought in and Blended  crude oil from at least 15 different  countries with an average of 30 percent  coming from Russia now the overwhelming  majority of it comes from Russia because  European Banks stopped lending money to  the isab refinery after Russia invaded  Ukraine  as a result the Lukoil Refinery was no  longer able to buy crude from other  countries  since April as much as 93 percent of its  feedstock is pure Russian crude  the rest is a Russian Kazakh Blend or  Kazakh according to data from commodity  tracking firm vortexa  all of the crude came from Russian ports  with the most coming from primorsk we  see a shipment loading there in this  satellite image from late March the  vessel here is the SCF baltica a tanker  belonging to Russia's largest  state-owned Shipping Company a company  sanctioned by the U.S  self conflict did not respond to a  request for comment we see the same ship  again a few weeks later delivering that  oil to the refinery in Sicily  it's this crucial stop that eventually  allows Luke oil to sell this oil as  gasoline and other products to the U.S  a spokeswoman for the U.S department of  the treasury which administers and  enforces sanctions declined to comment  but U.S sanctions State an exception for  any Russian origin good that has been  substantially transformed into a  foreign-made product  in general substantial transformation  means that the oil has been changed  through processing or manufacturing such  as refining  refining is a process in which crude is  heated and distilled  while this process technically  transforms the crude into a different  product if the same raw ingredients used  in Luke oil's Sicily Refinery were  brought to the U.S it would be a  violation of sanctions  the refinery exported almost 5 million  barrels of oil products to the us since  March of this year roughly 2.5 million  of those barrels were gasoline  according to the American petroleum  Institute that's enough to fill about 7  million automobile gas tanks  we traced shipments delivered to at  least seven different buyers in at least  13 different locations  some of this was gasoline while we can  see where it was delivered shipping data  doesn't always identify the buyer  data shows fuel oil and gasoline were  purchased by Exxon and oil trading  companies such as lukoil's own trading  arm latasca  the most oil products sent from Luke oil  Sicily to the us since March went to  exxon's terminal in Baytown Texas just  outside Houston  nearby a terminal owned by Magellan  Midstream Partners a company that  transports and distributes oil products  received the largest amount of gasoline  a spokesperson for Magellan said  although they received the shipment they  were not the buyer and shipping data  does not list one  another gasoline shipment over 300 000  barrels arrived at this terminal in New  Jersey  shipping data indicates the receiver for  that delivery was also Exxon  a spokeswoman from Exxon said Exxon  Mobil is complying with all sanctions  and the deliveries it received are  certified products of Italian origin  Luke oil did not respond to multiple  requests for comment  though shipments like these from Luke  oil Sicily Refinery may represent only a  fraction of U.S oil imports they reveal  how difficult it is to track and  eliminate Russian oil in the U.S supply  chain the local refinery in Sicily will  be forced to stop importing Russian  crude and may have to close when  European sanctions Banning it come into  effect on December 5th  in the meantime at a number of gas  stations across the U.S Americans are  filling up their vehicles paying for  fuel that in part adds to Russia's  profits  foreign
 

